WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:06.670
This video is sponsored by Brilliant — start
learning today with the link in the description.

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:13.480
Among fast-food chains, Starbucks is unique
in the way that it dominates mainstream culture.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:19.700
Not only in the language it’s trained us
all to use — Tall, Grande, Venti, etc.,

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:22.790
But also in the scrutiny it receives…

00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:26.710
No other cup design can command such controversy,

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:31.070
No other change of store policy gets so much
media attention,

00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:36.999
And no other drink has taken on such a life
of its own as the Pumpkin Spice Latte.

00:00:36.999 --> 00:00:44.089
Mocked, stereotyped, and yet still highly
adored, Starbucks is known everywhere as the

00:00:44.089 --> 00:00:47.809
coffeeshop — almost in a league of its own.

00:00:47.809 --> 00:00:53.200
But what you might not know is that, viewed
from a financial perspective, Starbucks is

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:59.440
far more than a seller of expensive drinks
— Starbucks has become a bank.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:04.720
Whether you know it or not, Starbucks might
already be your bank.

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:10.640
Its customers have lent the company around
$1.6 billion dollars.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:16.920
In some ways, Starbucks is a technology company,
competing (favorably) with Paypal.

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:21.380
Even some traditional banks are fearful of
what it might do next.

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:22.560
Why?

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:26.939
How did Starbucks, behind all of our backs,
become a bank?

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:34.479
Arguably the first genius idea in Starbuck
history was to carefully craft an experience

00:01:34.479 --> 00:01:40.149
— its physical locations would be just as
much an attraction as its coffee.

00:01:40.149 --> 00:01:45.560
More than just a restaurant though, each coffeehouse
was designed to act as a “third place”

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:50.630
in our lives — something that would complement
both the rigid, formal setting that is the

00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:54.090
workplace and our private homes.

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:55.880
It was well-timed.

00:01:55.880 --> 00:02:01.500
As it grew during the 90s and early 2000s,
the number of “shared spaces” which served

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:06.270
a communal, social, inclusive role were in
decline.

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:12.430
Fewer malls, for example, were being built,
and many sat empty or forgotten.

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:18.159
At the same time, more people were becoming
self-employed or had begun working from home,

00:02:18.159 --> 00:02:23.970
creating the need for a place with good WiFi,
ample caffeine, and air conditioning.

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:29.780
Tacky as it may sound, Starbucks was also
supposed to be friendly — in a small-town

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:35.900
neighborhood kind of way, encouraging its
employees to get to know regulars, make small

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:41.870
talk, and personalize each cup with its customer’s
name — misspelled as they may be.

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:47.510
Besides these reasons, Starbucks grew thanks
to its great branding, remarkable consistency,

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:52.210
and, yes, arguably, the taste of its coffee.

00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:57.540
Most impressively, it expanded very rapidly
without franchising.

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:02.409
The company believed, from the very beginning,
that it needed to exert very tight control

00:03:02.409 --> 00:03:06.519
over every process and facet of its presentation.

00:03:06.519 --> 00:03:10.360
It was also very good at scouting locations.

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:15.799
So much so that there’s a so-called “Frappuccino
Effect” in real estate — a significant

00:03:15.799 --> 00:03:20.550
increase in nearby property prices when a
new location opens.

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:27.540
According to the CEO of Zillow, in the time
a normal home has appreciated by 65%, one

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:33.079
near a Starbucks location will have appreciated
by 96%.

00:03:33.079 --> 00:03:39.709
This is likely due in part to the perceived
value of its convenience, but also, in large

00:03:39.709 --> 00:03:46.210
part, to Starbucks’ ability to find up-and-coming
neighborhoods before the rest of the market.

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:50.510
The very first location opened in Seattle
in 1971,

00:03:50.510 --> 00:03:58.290
There were 84 locations in 1990,
677 in 1995,

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:02.930
The first international, in Japan, during
1996,

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:11.049
3,501 by the turn of the millennium,
And 16,680 in 2008.

00:04:11.049 --> 00:04:16.090
At its peak, over 7 new stores were opening
each day.

00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:22.320
Around this time, however, Starbucks began
showing signs that it had grown too big, too

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:23.320
fast.

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:28.000
Some in the company attributed these issues
to the wider global recession, but it was

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:34.640
hard to ignore the increasing competition
from McDonald’s, the concern about oversaturation,

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:37.560
and rising customer dissatisfaction.

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:42.919
This culminated when Howard Schultz, a board
member at the time, wrote a brutally honest

00:04:42.919 --> 00:04:46.870
internal memo that was somehow leaked to the
press.

00:04:46.870 --> 00:04:53.440
The email openly questions its focus on efficiency
and speed over quality, and suggests, politely,

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:56.810
that the company had lost its “magic”.

00:04:56.810 --> 00:04:58.620
It was time for a change.

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:04.620
If Starbucks was to continue being, Starbucks,
it needed to do something drastic.

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:06.520
And that, it did.

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:12.430
Schultz, who wrote the leaked memo, was effectively
the founder of modern Starbucks — taking

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:19.530
over the brand in 1982, back when it was just
four little coffeehouses in the Pacific Northwest.

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:24.830
He stepped down in 2000 to devote his time
to international expansion, with a focus on

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:26.100
China.

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:32.290
But in the rocky times of 2008, Schultz was
asked to return to the position of CEO.

00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:37.620
He inherited the company’s worst 3-month
performance in its history, and was tasked

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:40.810
with guiding it back to its glory days.

00:05:40.810 --> 00:05:47.020
After just one month in his new role, Shultz
made a drastic decision: Things had gotten

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:52.770
so sloppy, he needed to make a big gesture
to reinvigorate the company.

00:05:52.770 --> 00:05:58.750
He decided to close every single store for
one day, at an estimated cost of $6 million

00:05:58.750 --> 00:06:01.229
dollars in lost profit.

00:06:01.229 --> 00:06:08.280
That day, its 135,000 baristas, at the time,
were reminded of the very basics — how to

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:13.699
pour coffee, please customers, and pay attention
to the details.

00:06:13.699 --> 00:06:19.540
Later that year, a similar all-hands-on-board
meeting was called at the corporate level.

00:06:19.540 --> 00:06:25.160
10,000 of its partners were flown to New Orleans
for an additional $30 million, where they

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:28.680
were updated during a 3-day conference.

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:31.740
And then came the really hard part.

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:38.750
Starbucks closed 600 stores in 2008, followed
by 300 more in 2009.

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:44.260
It did, on the other hand, continue offering
free online college to every employee working

00:06:44.260 --> 00:06:49.450
more than 20 hours a week, in partnership
with Arizona State University.

00:06:49.450 --> 00:06:55.360
Among quick-serve restaurants, jobs at Starbucks
are highly coveted and see lower than average

00:06:55.360 --> 00:06:56.860
turnover.

00:06:56.860 --> 00:07:02.520
One of the less high profile changes was an
increasing focus on technology.

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:06.569
This began with the introduction of the Starbucks
Card.

00:07:06.569 --> 00:07:12.770
For the uninitiated, the way it works now,
in 2021, is this: Instead of paying with cash

00:07:12.770 --> 00:07:16.720
or credit card, you can also add money to
your Starbucks account.

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:21.569
Then, you can pay with the app on your phone,
giving you twice the number of “stars”,

00:07:21.569 --> 00:07:24.250
which let you redeem free drinks.

00:07:24.250 --> 00:07:30.330
While this may not sound all that revolutionary,
Starbucks is the most popular restaurant rewards

00:07:30.330 --> 00:07:34.349
app, allowing it to do what others can’t...

00:07:34.349 --> 00:07:39.010
Because of its size and customer loyalty,
people are not afraid to keep decent chunks

00:07:39.010 --> 00:07:43.000
of their money in their Starbucks account,
knowing they’ll use it eventually.

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:48.570
41% of U.S. and Canadian users pay with their
Starbucks card.

00:07:48.570 --> 00:07:56.039
At the end of 2019, users held a collective
$1.5 billion dollars in balances.

00:07:56.039 --> 00:08:02.610
To put this in perspective, 85% of all U.S.
banks have less than $1 billion dollars in

00:08:02.610 --> 00:08:05.050
total assets.

00:08:05.050 --> 00:08:10.680
Customers will, of course, eventually exchange
this money for coffee, but in the meantime,

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:17.600
unknowingly provide Starbucks with a $1.5
billion dollar loan, at 0% interest.

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:23.479
That’s money Starbucks cou ld simply invest
in the market, earning free money from the

00:08:23.479 --> 00:08:27.240
“generosity” of its users, or spend on
building more stores.

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:30.160
Actually, it’s even better than that.

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:36.460
About 10% of this money will be forgotten
or lost and never used — known in the industry

00:08:36.460 --> 00:08:38.120
as breakage.

00:08:38.120 --> 00:08:43.070
But here’s the thing: Starbucks is not a
bank, legally speaking.

00:08:43.070 --> 00:08:49.430
With only a few exceptions, Starbucks balance
cannot be withdrawn for cash, only coffee,

00:08:49.430 --> 00:08:55.560
allowing it to bypass financial regulation
and use deposited money however it likes.

00:08:55.560 --> 00:09:00.540
It doesn’t, for example, have to keep a
certain amount of cash ready on hand in case

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:02.810
of mass withdrawals.

00:09:02.810 --> 00:09:09.190
If it wanted to, Starbucks has all the ingredients
necessary to build a fully-fledged currency

00:09:09.190 --> 00:09:15.400
or partner with other brands to create a widely-available
mobile payment system.

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:20.170
Customers already use Starbucks gift cards
as the next best thing to money — it’s

00:09:20.170 --> 00:09:25.160
the go-to gift to someone you don’t know
super well but don’t want to give cash.

00:09:25.160 --> 00:09:30.330
It’s sorta liquid — not just because it
can be converted to Coffee, but because you

00:09:30.330 --> 00:09:37.300
can be reasonably sure anyone will have use
for it, making it an almost universal intermediary.

00:09:37.300 --> 00:09:40.550
All this is very scary to regular old banks.

00:09:40.550 --> 00:09:47.220
The CEO of South Korea’s third-largest financial
group was quoted calling Starbucks “an unregulated

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:49.810
bank, not a mere coffee company”.

00:09:49.810 --> 00:09:56.320
It’s anyone’s guess what Starbucks will
do next, or what its ultimate goals are.

00:09:56.320 --> 00:10:01.330
In the meantime, though, no one can say it
hasn’t kept up with the times or managed

00:10:01.330 --> 00:10:04.250
an incredible comeback.

